WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.373
Please welcome Karrie Martin Lachney.

2
00:00:03.373 --> 00:00:06.500
(crowd applauds and cheers)

3
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:10.240
I am so excited to meet you.

4
00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.860
And I was just wondering, like,

5
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:16.460
how was it when you first came in,

6
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.330
America introduced you,

7
00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.680
saying that she brought you in to play this character,

8
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:25.293
but what was the experience like for you on your side?

9
00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:30.210
My gosh, from the start, I honestly didn't,

10
00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.480 line:15%
I didn't think I could play Ana Morales

11
00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.400 line:15%
because I had been in this industry for a few years

12
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.140 line:15%
with the idea of a stereotype

13
00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:44.360 line:15%
that the industry wanted to put on a Latina actress.

14
00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:45.740
And when I walked in

15
00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:48.850
and I didn't have to put on a fake accent

16
00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:49.700
or anything like that.

17
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:51.440
I remember Marvin being like -

18
00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.550
I was like, I'm sorry, I really can't do an accent,

19
00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.870
so I'm just going to give it to you like this.

20
00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.150
And he was like, you are enough.

21
00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:01.790
And those words have stayed with me for the last two years.

22
00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:05.283
It just really changed my mindset, my perspective.

23
00:01:05.283 --> 00:01:07.692
(audience applauds)

24
00:01:07.692 --> 00:01:08.525
It is -

25
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:14.503
It is shocking to me how the system can work.

26
00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:16.610
I can only imagine

27
00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:20.078
how refreshing it was to be encouraged to be yourself.

28
00:01:20.078 --> 00:01:21.220
(Karrie) Yeah.

29
00:01:21.220 --> 00:01:23.880
And I'm so glad that America

30
00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.200
provides those types of environments and shows.

31
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.270
That's at the core of it.

32
00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:32.370
I think what is so special about this project for us

33
00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.130
and the whole season two takes place in, you know,

34
00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:37.150
Pop being released from detention

35
00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:38.520
to like the decision, right?

36
00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.010
Will he get to stay or will he be deported?

37
00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:45.460
And the whole season is watching this family live life

38
00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:47.870
that yes, there's anxiety and fear and the unknown,

39
00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:50.530
but there's also humor and joy and laughter

40
00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:52.140
and a horrible Thanksgiving dinner.

41
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.650
Because life, you know happens

42
00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.150
and because we're full complex humans.

43
00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:58.130
We don't just sit around all day and be like,

44
00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:00.000
who's going to get deported today, you know.

45
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.600
Like that's maybe a part of the story for some of us,

46
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.890
but it's not the whole story. We're full humans.

47
00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:09.250
And we're like funny and sexy and awful too.

48
00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:12.010
I am such a romantic at heart

49
00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:14.580
and one of the things that really captivated me

50
00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:17.780
about you beautiful women

51
00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.230
was your love stories with your husbands.

52
00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:23.410
And I was just curious if you would tell us both

53
00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.030
about how you met them and a little bit about it,

54
00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:28.260
because I was so swept up in it.

55
00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:31.278
I've been with my husband 16 and a half years now.

56
00:02:31.278 --> 00:02:33.080
(Drew) And you met like making a student film together?

57
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:36.000
He was making, he graduated from the USC film school

58
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.160
and was making his first short film.

59
00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.160
And we met for a lunch because he, you know,

60
00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.080
he sent me the script and everything

61
00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:46.550
and I was like, oh, this is nice, but I'm tired,

62
00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:47.570
I'm probably not going to do this.

63
00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:50.060
And on the last page, there was like a press clipping.

64
00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:52.320
And I was like, ooh, who's that?

65
00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.090
It couldn't hurt to take a meeting,

66
00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.580
never hurts to know people.

67
00:02:57.580 --> 00:02:59.240
And then, and then I forgot about it.

68
00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:04.240
And then I showed up and we had a nine hour lunch.

69
00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.366
(audience applauds)

70
00:03:06.366 --> 00:03:09.460
I love this! Thank you for indulging me.

71
00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:13.030
I'm living vicariously through the beautiful romance

72
00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:15.240
that is not fantasy or television. It's reality.

73
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.545
It's real, yeah, yeah.

74
00:03:17.545 --> 00:03:18.941
(Karrie) I love that so much!

75
00:03:18.941 --> 00:03:19.774
Okay.

76
00:03:19.774 --> 00:03:23.140
So my husband and I met in the sixth grade

77
00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:26.810
and we loathed each other so much.

78
00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.780 line:15%
He picked on me, I picked on him.

79
00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:30.930 line:15%
And come eighth grade, I don't know,

80
00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:32.210 line:15%
we became really good friends.

81
00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:35.920 line:15%
I think that hatred became like,

82
00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.900
and then in high school we started dating

83
00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:43.440
against my father's will

84
00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.440
and just fast forward high school, college, I moved to LA.

85
00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:51.900
The long distance thing was not for us.

86
00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:55.310
So we didn't talk for about three years

87
00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:56.840
in that, like, distance.

88
00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:59.200
We started communicating over like Facebook

89
00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.970
and then he started coming to visit LA

90
00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:03.220
and that's -

91
00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:04.160
one thing led to another.

92
00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.660
And we were like engaged in like less than a year.

93
00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.355
I love it!

94
00:04:08.355 --> 00:04:11.372
(audience applauds)

95
00:04:11.372 --> 00:04:12.468
Really quickly,

96
00:04:12.468 --> 00:04:13.990
I know this isn't a really a quickly question,

97
00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:15.950
but I just have to ask you also,

98
00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:17.874
you were pregnant in a pandemic.

99
00:04:17.874 --> 00:04:18.980
(America) Yeah.

100
00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:20.730
How did you handle that?

101
00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:21.700
It was stressful.

102
00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:25.880
I was seven months pregnant when shutdowns began

103
00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:27.560
and it was all - we'd had to change everything

104
00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.070
because I was working in LA.

105
00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:30.640
The plan was to come back to New York

106
00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.540
and do everything that I'd already done with my son.

107
00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:36.610
I knew where I was. I'm like I did this, I got this.

108
00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.050
And then of course it's like the pandemic hit

109
00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:40.670
and we had to switch gears.

110
00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:42.680
We had to change doctors, change hospitals,

111
00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:43.880
change everything.

112
00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:47.340
But to do it without the like friends or family or help

113
00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.370
that you might normally have, it was very intense

114
00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:53.490
but you know, she's 18 months old now.

115
00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:54.490
(Drew) And like thriving.

116
00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.540
And just delicious.

117
00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:58.550
And she and her brother loved each other.

118
00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:01.319
And it's great. I mean -

119
00:05:01.319 --> 00:05:02.471
(audience applauds)

120
00:05:02.471 --> 00:05:05.410
I feel like every day,

121
00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:09.100
my goals are just about finding more joy in my life.

122
00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.550
Like we work.

123
00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:12.740
I mean, it's like, what did we take away from this pandemic,

124
00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:15.100
if not, but nothing is promised to us, right?

125
00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:16.550
I'm going to make myself cry.

126
00:05:17.690 --> 00:05:19.340
You know, nothing's promised to us.

127
00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:24.340
And I think we all need to be more gentle with ourselves.

128
00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:27.660
Be kinder to ourselves. If you do anything today,

129
00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.852
like just be a little kinder to yourself and,

130
00:05:30.852 --> 00:05:32.768
and find a little window

131
00:05:32.768 --> 00:05:36.980
to like experience a little bit more joy every day.

132
00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:38.684
That's where I'm at.

133
00:05:38.684 --> 00:05:41.684
(audience applauds)

134
00:05:43.399 --> 00:05:46.280 line:15%
Thank you. Thank you for that, you guys. I am -

135
00:05:49.027 --> 00:05:50.960 line:15%
I've been so looking forward to this conversation

136
00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:54.470
and Gentefied, which is on Netflix in season two,

137
00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:55.953
please be sure to watch it.

138
00:05:57.197 --> 00:05:59.670
And thank you so much, you guys for being here.

139
00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:00.530
We'll be right back.

140
00:06:00.530 --> 00:06:02.047
Thank you.

141
00:06:02.047 --> 00:06:04.630 line:15%
(fading music)

